\f0\b0\fs28 \cf0 Treat these results as 
\f1\b conversation starters, not conclusions
\f0\b0 . Scores are influenced by context, interpretation of questions, mood, culture, role demands, and self-perception. The most valuable insight comes from comparing the scores with real-world behavior, feedback from trusted colleagues, and patterns observed over time.\
Also be careful not to apply these labels to others. Respecting colleagues' privacy, dignity, and complexity is usually more informative than categorizing people. The goal is not to decide what type of person someone is, but to understand which conditions help everyone act at their best and which conditions increase the likelihood of unhelpful behaviors.\
